refactor!: drop the dbsc.enabled flag

The switch promised "false makes the whole feature a no-op", but that is not
what it did. It removed DbscFilter and DbscProofGuardFilter from the context,
so any application that wires those beans -- which is every application
following the README -- failed injection and refused to start:

  Parameter 0 of method dbscFilterRegistration required a bean of type
  'click.yukio.dbsc.web.DbscFilter' that could not be found.

So the flag could only ever do two things: nothing, or take the application
down. An operator reaching for it during an incident would have got the second.

The honest way to turn DBSC off is to remove the dependency. Both remaining
usages were dead branches in that shadow: the early return in bind() and the
requireEnabled() guard in the two native handlers, neither of which could be
reached in a context where the flag was false, because there was no DbscService
to call.

DbscProperties.isEnabled()/setEnabled() are removed, so this is a binary and
source break for anyone who called them directly. dbsc.bound is untouched --
that one is a real runtime decision between two working configurations.

Verified: 125 unit tests, 134 E2E checks against a live HTTPS demo, all 19
error codes still covered.
